---
import {stations, releaseCases, assessRelease, decisionLabels, FACTORY_BRIEF_PATH} from '../lib/software-factory';
import '../styles/software-factory.css';

interface Props {
  journeyId: string;
  releaseId: string;
  allocateId: (base: string) => string;
}
const {journeyId, releaseId, allocateId} = Astro.props;
const ids = new Map<string, string>();
const id = (name: string): string => {
  if (!ids.has(name)) ids.set(name, allocateId(`factory-${name}`));
  return ids.get(name)!;
};
const choices = ['hold', 'reconcile', 'advance', 'contain'] as const;
---

<div class="factory-experience">
  <section class="factory-order" aria-labelledby={id('order-title')}>
    <div class="factory-opening">
      <h2 id={id('order-title')}>How the kitchen idea works.</h2>
      <p>In a well-run kitchen, people can work independently because the standards, responsibilities and handoffs are clear. The finished dish still belongs to the team that serves it.</p>
      <p>Apply that idea to software: AI can carry a change through the whole lifecycle. Engineers define the promise, authorize the operating rules and remain responsible for the result.</p>
      <p class="factory-scope">An original architecture exploration using a Michelin kitchen analogy. The booking service and all evidence below are fictional. No agents or production systems run on this page.</p>
    </div>
    <aside class="factory-ticket" aria-labelledby={id('ticket-title')}>
      <p class="factory-ticket-context">The order · fictional booking service</p>
      <h3 id={id('ticket-title')}>Let a customer reschedule with confidence.</h3>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Keep consistent</dt><dd>The confirmed time on screen, in the calendar and in reminders.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Expect trouble</dt><dd>Retries, simultaneous changes and a reminder already in flight.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Responsible human</dt><dd>A named outcome owner, supported by engineering and service owners.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <details><summary>Open the acceptance examples</summary><ul><li>An eligible customer changes only their own booking.</li><li>A retried request does not apply the change twice.</li><li>An obsolete queued reminder is suppressed before the defined dispatch boundary.</li><li>The confirmation works with keyboard and assistive technology.</li><li>Already submitted messages follow an explicit recovery and communication policy.</li></ul></details>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="factory-journey" id={journeyId} aria-labelledby={id('journey-title')}>
    <div class="factory-section-heading"><h2 id={id('journey-title')}>Follow the change.</h2><p>For a quick tour, inspect Verify and Operate, then <a href={`#${releaseId}`}>make a release decision</a>. Open any handoff for the evidence it needs.</p></div>
    <fieldset class="factory-stations" id={id('handoffs')} aria-describedby={id('station-help')}>
      <legend class="visually-hidden">Seven handoffs from request to continuing ownership</legend>
      <p id={id('station-help')} class="factory-control-help">Choose a stage. Arrow keys also work when the group is focused. These are fixed explanatory views.</p>
      <div class="factory-station-selector">
        {stations.map((station, index) => <>
          <input class:list={['factory-radio', `station-choice-${station.key}`]} type="radio" name={id('station-group')} id={id(`station-${station.key}`)} aria-controls={id(`station-panel-${station.key}`)} checked={index === 0} />
          <label class="factory-station-label" for={id(`station-${station.key}`)}><span aria-hidden="true">{index + 1}</span><span>{station.short}</span><span class="visually-hidden">: {station.name}</span></label>
        </>)}
        {stations.map((station, index) => <section class:list={['factory-station', `station-panel-${station.key}`]} id={id(`station-panel-${station.key}`)} aria-labelledby={id(`station-title-${station.key}`)}>
          <div class="factory-station-work inverse">
            <p class="factory-station-position">Handoff {index + 1} of {stations.length} · {station.name}</p>
            <h3 id={id(`station-title-${station.key}`)}>{station.title}</h3>
            <p class="factory-stage-action">{station.action}</p>
            <div class="factory-worker"><h4>AI can do the work</h4><p>{station.agent}</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="factory-station-contract">
            <div class="factory-human"><h4>A human owns the judgment</h4><p>{station.human}</p></div>
            <details class="factory-handoff"><summary>{station.artifact}: inspect the handoff</summary><p>{station.handoff}</p><dl class="factory-evidence"><div><dt>This establishes</dt><dd>{station.evidence}</dd></div><div><dt>Still open</dt><dd>{station.open}</dd></div></dl></details>
          </div>
          <details class="factory-station-trap"><summary>{station.check}</summary><p>{station.answer}</p></details>
          <div class="factory-stage-return"><a href={`#${id('handoffs')}`}>Choose another stage</a><a href={`#${releaseId}`}>Try the release decisions</a></div>
        </section>)}
      </div>
    </fieldset>
    <p class="factory-journey-note">The route is drawn as a sequence to make the handoffs clear. Real work loops back, runs in parallel and waits for evidence. Every consequential transition still needs its own current authority.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="factory-release" id={releaseId} aria-labelledby={id('release-title')}>
    <div class="factory-section-heading"><h2 id={id('release-title')}>Your turn at the release pass.</h2><p>The pass is where a kitchen checks the whole dish before service. Here, a limited trial rollout, called a canary, has been submitted. Decide what happens before more customers receive it.</p></div>
    <fieldset class="factory-cases">
      <legend>Choose a release situation</legend>
      <div class="factory-case-selector">
        {releaseCases.map((scenario, index) => <>
          <input class:list={['factory-radio', `case-choice-${scenario.key}`]} type="radio" name={id('case-group')} id={id(`case-${scenario.key}`)} aria-controls={id(`case-panel-${scenario.key}`)} checked={index === 0} />
          <label class="factory-case-label" for={id(`case-${scenario.key}`)}>{scenario.label}</label>
        </>)}
        {releaseCases.map(scenario => {
          const result = assessRelease(scenario.facts);
          return <section class:list={['factory-case', `case-panel-${scenario.key}`]} id={id(`case-panel-${scenario.key}`)} aria-labelledby={id(`case-title-${scenario.key}`)}>
            <div class="factory-case-evidence"><p class="factory-case-context">Illustrative release evidence</p><h3 id={id(`case-title-${scenario.key}`)}>{scenario.title}</h3><ul>{scenario.evidence.map(fact => <li>{fact}</li>)}</ul></div>
            <fieldset class="factory-call"><legend>What would you do next?</legend><div class="factory-call-options">
              {choices.map(choice => <>
                <input class:list={['factory-radio', `call-choice-${choice}`]} type="radio" name={id(`call-group-${scenario.key}`)} id={id(`call-${scenario.key}-${choice}`)} aria-controls={id(`feedback-${scenario.key}-${choice}`)} />
                <label class="factory-call-label" for={id(`call-${scenario.key}-${choice}`)}>{decisionLabels[choice]}</label>
              </>)}
              {choices.map(choice => <div class:list={['factory-feedback', `call-feedback-${choice}`, {'factory-feedback-match': choice === result.decision}]} id={id(`feedback-${scenario.key}-${choice}`)}>
                <h4>{choice === result.decision ? 'That matches the illustrated policy.' : `The illustrated policy calls for: ${decisionLabels[result.decision].toLowerCase()}.`}</h4>
                <p>{scenario.lesson}</p>
                <details><summary>Inspect the policy reasoning</summary><ul>{result.reasons.map(reason => <li>{reason}</li>)}</ul></details>
              </div>)}
            </div></fieldset>
          </section>;
        })}
      </div>
    </fieldset>
    <p class="factory-scope">A small, build-time teaching model drives these answers. It is not a complete release policy, security assessment or authorization to deploy. The engineering notes below explain what a real implementation still needs.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="factory-continuation" aria-labelledby={id('continue-title')}>
    <h2 id={id('continue-title')}>Now look under the hood.</h2>
    <div><p>The kitchen makes the responsibilities visible. Software adds concurrency, adversarial inputs, sensitive data and effects that can outlive a release. Those need engineering contracts.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="text-link" href="#approach">Read the architecture and its limits</a><a class="text-link" href={FACTORY_BRIEF_PATH} download="fidret-software-factory-brief.md">Take the operating brief</a></div><p class="factory-download-note">Editable Markdown. Keep your completed copy in your own approved workspace.</p></div>
  </section>
</div>
